Bianbu Agent Runtime for Debian/Ubuntu
======================================

Building from source
--------------------

A clean checkout can be built directly with:

    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

When `SECURITY_MANIFEST_DIR` is not supplied, the build creates an ephemeral
Ed25519 key in memory and emits a development self-signed Runtime manifest.
The private key is never written to disk or included in the package. The DEB
contains `/usr/share/doc/bianbu-agent-runtime/DEVELOPMENT-SELF-SIGNED` so it
cannot be mistaken for an officially signed release artifact.

Release builds must supply a complete production manifest explicitly:

    SECURITY_MANIFEST_DIR=/secure/release-manifest \
        dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

An explicit manifest directory is validated and never replaced by a generated
development manifest.

The package contains its locked Python dependencies under
`/usr/lib/bianbu-agent-runtime`. The packaged profile includes a preconfigured
built-in Bibit Provider and a starter Agent template for this internal deployment. The
Provider is published as the Custom Provider `custom:bibit` (shown as "Bibit Built-in") so the
Models page never offers it as a vendor template the user is expected to fill in. The DEB
and every installed copy contain that API credential, so distribute the package only to
trusted recipients and rotate the key if the package leaves that boundary.

System Runtime service
------------------------

The package installs one system-level service, `bianbu-agent-runtime.service`,
wanted by `multi-user.target`: the Runtime starts at boot, independently of who
logs in. Its identity is the device user `postinst` resolves once; the account is
recorded in `/etc/bianbu-agent-runtime/service-user` and published as the drop-in
`/etc/systemd/system/bianbu-agent-runtime.service.d/10-service-user.conf`, because
`User=` cannot read a configuration file. Running as the device user rather than a
dedicated system account is deliberate: host Workers are real login sessions and
need a home directory, the user's files, and normal sudo/PAM semantics. Host and
bwrap Workers keep using system transient scopes, and the polkit rule admits those
scopes only when the caller lives in `bianbu-agent-runtime.service` and delegates
nothing else; the console is opened in a browser at the Runtime port, so no
in-session caller has to start that unit. A package install that cannot see a
device user (an image build in a chroot, a container or CI) keeps the packaged
default of the dedicated `bianbu-runtime` account instead of failing, and does not
enable the unit there.

The package depends on `bianbu-agent-securityd (>= 0.1.18)`. The package
transaction itself only writes files: an image build may run it in a chroot, a
container or CI, where no systemd manager, no D-Bus and no securityd exist yet.
Everything that needs the live device happens when the service starts, through
`bianbu-agent-runtime-register.service`, a oneshot unit that the Runtime and
the network-learning collector pull in via `Requires=`/`After=`.
It grants the `bianbu-security` membership, materialises the live `/run` state,
resets only the stable `com.bianbu.runtime` caller binding through securityd's
audited administrator API, and publishes the signed Runtime manifest. A
half-installed image therefore registers itself on first boot; if a security
step fails there, the Runtime refuses to start and `capabilities.db` is never
deleted.

Useful commands are:

    sudo systemctl start bianbu-agent-runtime.service
    sudo systemctl enable --now bianbu-agent-runtime.service
    sudo systemctl stop bianbu-agent-runtime.service
    systemctl status bianbu-agent-runtime.service
    journalctl -u bianbu-agent-runtime.service

Callers do not have to guess which account owns the data directory. Resolution
order is `BIANBU_AGENT_CONFIG`, then the account recorded in
`/etc/bianbu-agent-runtime/service-user` and its `~/.bianbu-agent/config.yaml`,
then the caller's own `$HOME` when there is no record at all (a source checkout).
The `bianbu-agent` CLI and the Workers all follow it, so
`sudo bianbu-agent ...` never reads root's empty directory; a local user who is
not the service user gets told which account owns it instead of a bare
permission error. `/api/v1/runtime/status` reports `service_user` and `data_dir`
for the same reason, and the console's Runtime status page shows both.

Service start creates the service user's `~/.bianbu-agent`. The console port is
derived from that account's UID - 9899 for UID 1000, then
`9899 + ((uid - 1000) % 55637)` - so anyone can reach the console at
`http://<device>:<port>/` even though the service account's home is not readable
to them. The package ships no desktop entry, launcher or hicolor icon any more:
the console is opened in a browser at that port. A copy of the retired entry
left on a user's desktop - the retired installer's `Bianbu Agent.desktop`, or
`bianbu-agent.desktop` copied from the start menu while that entry existed - is
removed by every package transaction, because a byte-identical copy is what both
produce; an edited copy is left alone. The comparison reference is the packaged
`/usr/lib/bianbu-agent-runtime/retired-bianbu-agent.desktop`, which is not an
application-menu entry.

Package upgrades stop the service in `preinst` (along with any retired per-user
`bianbu-agent-runtime@*.service` instances), then `postinst` enables and starts the
shared unit again, which runs device registration first. The registration unit keeps a
digest of the installed manifest in
`/var/lib/bianbu-agent-runtime/registered-manifest.sha256`: an unchanged
generation only repairs a missing registration, while a new generation calls
securityd's administrator-only `ResetAgentBinding` operation before registering
the new signed manifest. That reset is what stops an upgraded Runtime from
inheriting the retired caller binding of the previous process identity. A
failed registration leaves the service stopped instead of falling back to a
generation whose binding is gone; `capabilities.db` is never deleted.

Installing without a live device
--------------------------------

An image build (pbuilder, a plain chroot, a container, CI or a firmware build)
has no running systemd, no securityd on the system bus and no image-provided
`bianbu` group, so `dpkg --configure` there can only lay the package down on
disk. No switch is needed:

    sudo apt install ./bianbu-agent-runtime_*.deb

The transaction never fails on a missing live prerequisite. It creates the
`bianbu-runtime` account, its data directory and the admin token, because they
are image content rather than live state, and it asks systemd to reload and to
start the service with every return value ignored; the unit is only enabled when
a device user was found. When securityd's `bianbu-security` group does not exist
yet it is left alone: the registration unit grants the membership on the device,
where securityd is present. Resetting the caller binding, registering the
manifest, creating live `/run` state and starting the service all wait for the
device. Nothing is copied onto a user's desktop, and `systemctl` diagnostics from
a build host are routed to `/dev/null`, so a firmware build stays quiet.

To register and start an image that was only unpacked on a build host, enable the
service on the device and start it; the registration unit runs first:

    sudo systemctl enable --now bianbu-agent-runtime.service

The registration unit never registers a Runtime whose binding could not be
reset: on a device it fails closed with
`securityd is unavailable; refusing to start the Runtime` instead of starting an
unregistered Runtime.

Packaged components and upgrades
--------------------------------

The package profile supplies the tracked Agents, Tools, Skills, Hooks, Plugins,
MCP and Model definitions from the source `.bianbu-agent` profile. On the first
service start they are copied into the corresponding
directories below `~/.bianbu-agent`. When the packaged profile changes, all
package-managed component IDs are replaced, including locally edited copies.
Changed or retired copies are saved first below:

    ~/.bianbu-agent/backups/package-upgrade/<profile-digest>/

Components created by the user under IDs that the package has never managed
are preserved. When the packaged profile changes during an upgrade, the Runtime's
`config.yaml` and all package-managed components are replaced
from the packaged profile, including the LAN listening address, port, Bibit
soul and built-in model selection. The packaged Bibit
Provider under `models/custom-bibit` is also replaced from the DEB profile. Previous
config, Agents, Providers and other managed files are saved under the same
backup directory before replacement. User-created Agents and components whose
IDs have never been supplied by the package, plus sessions, session logs and workspace,
are not replaced. Removing the DEB does not remove the Runtime data or its
backups.

An existing `config.yaml` is the user's file: provisioning only adds the
device's `presets` section to it when the profile declares one and the file has
none, and never touches the other keys. Delete `config.yaml` to get the
packaged profile back verbatim.

The profile keeps `agents`, `models`, `sessions`, `workspace`,
`logs`, `runtime` and `backups`. Each Session owns
`sessions/<session-id>.json` plus its transcript under
`sessions/<session-id>/session.jsonl.zstd`. Extension roots (`skills`,
`plugins`, `mcps`) are created on demand when an extension is actually
installed, so an untouched profile holds no empty directories for them.
Standalone Hook packages are retired: a Plugin provides its Hooks as package
members, so there is no `hooks` root.

Model setup
-----------

The first launch creates one ordinary starter Agent with a persisted random
`com.bianbu.<12-hex>` ID, bound to the packaged
`custom:bibit / deepseek-flash` model. The service and control panel can therefore be
used immediately. The package owns `models/custom-bibit/provider.yaml`; the first
edit is copied into the Runtime user's
`~/.bianbu-agent/models/custom-bibit/provider.yaml` with mode `0600`, and every later
edit updates that user copy, so the packaged credential is never modified in place.

1. Open **Configure provider** if you want to replace the packaged Bibit
   credential or Base URL.
2. Open **Select model** on the selected Agent if you want to change its model.

Runtime Provider credentials are stored in each user's
`~/.bianbu-agent/models` files with private permissions. They are never
returned in API responses or written to logs; this DEB is the explicit
exception because it intentionally embeds the deployment credential.

Open the packaged Runtime URL in a browser. Manual CLI use can still select another
configuration with `BIANBU_AGENT_CONFIG` or `--config /path/to/config.yaml`.

Runtime management API credential
---------------------------------

The package retains the root-only token management command for deployments
that enable the optional management API credential. The default Runtime does
not enable that gate, so opening the desktop console requires no token.

Retrieve the token on the board and paste it into the browser prompt:

    sudo bianbu-agent-runtime-token --get-token

To rotate it (the shared Runtime service is restarted automatically):

    sudo bianbu-agent-runtime-token --rotate

If a deployment enables token authentication, reload the user's Runtime URL
and enter the new token.
